---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PaperList from '../components/PaperList.astro';
import { paper } from '../data/publications';
import cheops from '../assets/figures/research-cheops.jpg';
import speculoosDusk from '../assets/photos/speculoos-dusk.jpg';
import talk from '../assets/photos/talk-exoplanets5.jpg';
import vlt from '../assets/photos/vlt.jpg';
import whtDome from '../assets/photos/wht-dome-opening.jpg';

// Every section has the same shape: a plain-English opening, a short account of my part,
// the key papers, and a picture only where the section is about what it shows.
const figSizes = '(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 40px), 420px';
---

<Base
  title="Research · Dr Matthew Hooton"
  description="Research by Dr Matthew Hooton: how hot Jupiters reached their orbits, small planets with JWST, and work with SPECULOOS and CHEOPS."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Research</h1>
      <nav class="subnav" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#hot-jupiters">How hot Jupiters arrived</a>
        <a href="#jwst">Small planets with JWST</a>
        <a href="#speculoos">SPECULOOS</a>
        <a href="#cheops">CHEOPS</a>
        <a href="#qub">The QUB eclipse campaign</a>
        <a href="#supervision">Supervision and mentoring</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section class="block research" id="hot-jupiters" aria-labelledby="hot-jupiters-h">
      <h2 id="hot-jupiters-h">How hot Jupiters arrived</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        Hot Jupiters are giant planets that orbit very close to their stars, and they probably
        moved there from further out. The tilt of a planet's orbit, compared with its star's
        equator, is a clue to how that happened. I measure the tilt from the shape of the dip in
        starlight as the planet passes in front of its star.
      </p>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="detail">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              The leading explanation is high-eccentricity migration. A giant planet that formed a
              few times further from its star than Earth is from the Sun is knocked onto an
              elongated orbit, and tides raised at each close approach to the star then shrink the
              orbit and make it circular. Each way of knocking a planet inwards tilts its orbit by
              a characteristic amount, and many hot Jupiters, especially around hot, fast-spinning
              stars, orbit far out of line with their star's equator. Young stars, whose planets
              have only just formed, show these processes closest to their start.
            </p>
            <p>
              Fast-spinning stars offer a direct way to measure the tilt. Their rotation makes them
              bulge at the equator, which is then cooler and dimmer than the poles: an effect called
              gravity darkening. A planet crossing such a star blocks a different amount of light at
              each point along its path, so the shape of the transit records the true angle between
              the orbit and the star's equator, not just its projection on the sky. The signal is
              subtle, and the result depends on how carefully the light curve is modelled.
            </p>
            <p>
              With light curves from CHEOPS and Spitzer, I confirmed that the hot Jupiter MASCARA-1 b
              has a near-polar orbit, and showed that observing the transit in two colours makes
              the measurement far more reliable. I am now analysing all the existing multicolour
              light curves of planets crossing fast-spinning stars in one consistent way, to look
              for patterns in their tilts and orbital shapes that point to how they migrated.
              JWST's light curves are about ten times more precise, so the same method now works
              for smaller planets.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="key-papers">
            <h3>Key paper</h3>
            <PaperList papers={[paper('spiops')]} compact />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block research" id="jwst" aria-labelledby="jwst-h">
      {/* The section's old anchor, so earlier links still land here. */}
      <span id="resonant-planets"></span>
      <h2 id="jwst-h">Small planets with JWST</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        TOI-178 has six known planets, and five of them orbit in step with each other. This
        pattern, called a resonance chain, is easily broken, so the planets have probably not been
        disturbed since they formed. The gases in their atmospheres can therefore show where each
        one formed.
      </p>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="detail">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              I was part of the team that found the six planets in 2021. They range from just
              larger than Earth to almost three times its size, and five of them form a chain of
              Laplace resonances, like the one linking three of Jupiter's moons. I lead a JWST
              programme that observed three of the planets with the NIRSpec spectrograph. Water,
              carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide freeze out at different distances from a young
              star, so their proportions in each atmosphere show whether the planet formed where
              it is now or moved there.
            </p>
            <p>
              With the same instrument, Chloe Fisher and I looked for an atmosphere on TOI-1685 b,
              a hot rocky planet. We found no clear sign of one: its spectrum fits a bare rock or
              an atmosphere of heavy molecules.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="key-papers">
            <h3>Key papers</h3>
            <PaperList
              papers={[paper('toi1685b-jwst'), paper('toi1685b-params'), paper('toi178')]}
              compact
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig">
          <Picture
            src={talk}
            alt="Matthew Hooton presenting JWST results on stage at the Exoplanets 5 conference"
            formats={['avif', 'webp']}
            widths={[480, 720, 960, 1280]}
            sizes={figSizes}
          />
          <figcaption class="cap">Presenting JWST results at Exoplanets 5, Leiden, 2024.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block research" id="speculoos" aria-labelledby="speculoos-h">
      <h2 id="speculoos-h">SPECULOOS</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        Earth-sized planets are easier to find around the smallest stars, because a small planet
        blocks a larger share of a small star's light. SPECULOOS uses robotic telescopes to watch
        nearby small, cool stars for these planets. The planets it finds are good targets for
        studying the atmospheres of rocky worlds with JWST.
      </p>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="detail">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              SPECULOOS is a network of robotic one-metre telescopes, led by the University of
              Liège with Cambridge, Birmingham, MIT, Bern and ETH Zurich as partners. It succeeds
              TRAPPIST, whose smaller telescopes found the seven Earth-sized planets of TRAPPIST-1.
            </p>
            <p>
              I develop the pipeline that turns each night's images from the SPECULOOS telescopes
              into the light curves used to find planets. The data are kept in an archive at
              Cambridge. The pipeline has made possible two SPECULOOS planet discoveries and the
              confirmation of dozens of planets first found by TESS.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="key-papers">
            <h3>Key papers</h3>
            <PaperList papers={[paper('speculoos3'), paper('spirit'), paper('toi2407b')]} compact />
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig">
          <Picture
            src={speculoosDusk}
            alt="Three SPECULOOS telescope domes open at dusk in the Atacama Desert"
            formats={['avif', 'webp']}
            widths={[480, 720, 960, 1280]}
            sizes={figSizes}
          />
          <figcaption class="cap">SPECULOOS South Observatory, Chile.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block research" id="cheops" aria-labelledby="cheops-h">
      <h2 id="cheops-h">CHEOPS</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        CHEOPS is a space telescope that measures the sizes of planets we already know about.
        Together with a planet's mass, its size gives its density. The density shows whether the
        planet is mostly rock, gas or water.
      </p>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="detail">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              CHEOPS, which ESA launched in 2019, watches bright, nearby stars already known to
              have planets. I am on its science team. I led its largest observing programme, which
              refined the sizes of small planets in systems with several planets, and I have led
              the analysis of CHEOPS data in papers on new multi-planet systems and on the
              atmospheres of hot Jupiters. I also coordinate the consortium's proposals for JWST
              time to follow up its most interesting planets.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="key-papers">
            <h3>Key papers</h3>
            <PaperList papers={[paper('wasp189b'), paper('nu2-lupi-d')]} compact />
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig">
          <Picture
            src={cheops}
            alt="An artist's impression of the CHEOPS satellite in front of a star with transiting planets"
            formats={['avif', 'webp']}
            widths={[400, 640, 960]}
            sizes={figSizes}
          />
          <figcaption class="cap">The CHEOPS satellite. Source: ESA.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block research" id="qub" aria-labelledby="qub-h">
      <h2 id="qub-h">The QUB eclipse campaign</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        When a planet passes behind its star, the light from the system dips slightly, because the
        planet's own light is hidden. The size of the dip shows how much light the planet gives off
        and reflects. During my PhD, I measured these dips for very hot giant planets, using
        telescopes on the ground.
      </p>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="detail">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              At Queen's University Belfast I designed and led a campaign to observe these eclipses
              at wavelengths that Hubble and Spitzer did not routinely cover. I won telescope time
              as principal investigator on the VLT, Gemini-North and other telescopes, coordinated a
              team of QUB astrophysicists to carry out the observations, analysed the data with
              software I wrote, and worked with theorists to interpret the results.
            </p>
            <p>
              I measured an upper limit of 181 parts per million on the depth of KELT-9b's eclipse
              in the near-ultraviolet U band, the first such measurement from the ground. It
              constrained the planet's thermal emission and how its temperature changes with
              height. Eclipses of WASP-12b in the i band hinted that a hot Jupiter's thermal
              emission can change over time. The campaign also included multicolour observations
              of WASP-103b and red-optical emission spectroscopy of KELT-16b and WASP-121b.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="key-papers">
            <h3>Key papers</h3>
            <PaperList papers={[paper('kelt9b-nuv'), paper('wasp12b-storms')]} compact />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="figs">
          <figure class="fig">
            <Picture
              src={whtDome}
              alt="The William Herschel Telescope's dome, with its shutter open, against an orange sunset sky"
              formats={['avif', 'webp']}
              widths={[480, 720, 960, 1280]}
              sizes={figSizes}
            />
            <figcaption class="cap">The William Herschel Telescope opening at sunset, La Palma.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <Picture
              src={vlt}
              alt="The Very Large Telescope's unit telescopes catching low sunlight at Paranal"
              formats={['avif', 'webp']}
              widths={[480, 720, 960, 1280]}
              sizes={figSizes}
            />
            <figcaption class="cap">The Very Large Telescope, Paranal, Chile.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block research" id="supervision" aria-labelledby="supervision-h">
      <h2 id="supervision-h">Supervision and mentoring</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I co-supervise Clàudia Janó Muñoz, a PhD student in Didier Queloz's group at Cambridge.
          She is developing the data pipeline for SPIRIT, a new near-infrared camera at SPECULOOS
          South, and improving its performance. Her first paper, on the warm Neptune TOI-2407 b,
          used observations from SPIRIT.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Queen's University Belfast, I helped to supervise a PhD student who studied the hot
          Jupiter WASP-12b with TESS.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also mentor school students in research, through a course in which they search NASA
          data for a new planet: <a href="/mentorship/">Exoplanet Research Mentorship</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
